  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain and gusty winds associated with the remnants\nof Hurricane Isaias continues tonight.\n\nRain continues to move into the area from the south, with the\nNorth Carolina mountains receiving the most rain thus far,\naround 1-3\"+. As such, still expecting coverage of rain to peak\nduring the overnight hours, and continuing into Sunday.\n\nIn general, much of the forecast area is likely to see 1-3 \ninches of rainfall, with higher totals expected in the NC\nmountains and along and east of the Blue Ridge due to upslope \nflow, and lower totals west of the Blue Ridge given a rain \nshadow effect. See the Hydro section below for more details on \nthe flooding potential.\n\nA very strong 850mb LLJ is expected to lead to a downslope wind\nevent for areas in SE West Virginia, SW Virginia, and NW North\nCarolina. May still have a few higher gusts above 3500ft \nreaching close to high wind warning criteria, but these should \nbe intermittent and isolated. Some of the higher elevation \nlocations, such as Banner Elk, NC, have already gusted over 50 \nmph so far today, and between 25 to 35 mph elsewhere in the \nregion. Wind gusts should diminish by late Sunday morning and \ninto the afternoon as the LLJ lifts northeast out of the region.\n\nBy Monday, all remnants of Isaias will be well east of the \nregion with just some lingering wrap around upslope showers for\nthe western slopes through late Monday morning. Dry through most\nof the upcoming week with possibly the next chance of rain by\nthe second half of next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Damaging winds and tornado threat on Sunday.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon, hi-res guidance is still showing high \npressure retreating enough to allow for a brief period of \ndestabilization along a triple point in central Virginia/north\ncentral North Carolina, which could pose a brief tornado \nthreat. Main source of uncertainty is how much clearing and thus\ndestabilization can occur. Some CAMs do show SBCAPE of 1000 J/kg\nby Sunday afternoon, despite continued precipitation and cloud\ncover. With a retreating wedge boundary and an influx of very\nmoist air, along with strong winds from the remnant tropical\nsystem, the environment still looks to be supportive of low \ntopped supercells, given 0-1km SRH exceeding 200m2/s2 and Bulk \nShear of 40-50kts.",
